.docname {Twin Contracts}
.include {docs}

`twin-schema` holds the two Kafka messages digital-twin and telemetry exchange when a building is registered, and the names of the topics that carry them. Before it, each message was hand-rolled as `json!` on one side and read as an untyped `serde_json::Value` on the other — the arrangement that produced the empty dashboard catalog that [Telemetry Schema](../services/telemetry.qd) was created to prevent.

---

## The exchange

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant T as digital-twin
        participant K as Kafka
        participant TS as telemetry

        T->>K: building-registration-requested<br/>RegistrationRequest
        K->>TS: consume, keyed by buildingId
        TS->>TS: upsert building + rooms, seed thresholds
        TS->>K: building-registration-completed<br/>RegistrationCompleted
        K->>T: consume, resolve the upload

Topic constants live beside the payload that travels on them — `telemetry-schema` owns `ALERTS_TOPIC` the same way. Each service re-exports both from its own `adapters/topics.rs`, so the string itself is written once:

```rust
// digital-twin/src/adapters/topics.rs
pub use twin_schema::{
    BUILDING_REGISTRATION_COMPLETED_TOPIC, BUILDING_REGISTRATION_REQUESTED_TOPIC,
};
```

Three services had previously declared these strings independently.

---

## 1. `RegistrationRequest` — leniency where it belongs

```rust
pub struct RegistrationRoom {
    pub id: String,
    pub name: String,
}

pub struct RegistrationRequest {
    pub building_id: Option<String>,
    pub name: String,
    pub max_temperature: Option<f64>,
    pub rooms: Vec<RegistrationRoom>,
}
```

The rooms list deserialises leniently, which is the behaviour telemetry already had as hand-written `Value` walking:

```rust
fn usable_rooms<'de, D>(deserializer: D) -> Result<Vec<RegistrationRoom>, D::Error> {
    let entries = Option::<Vec<Value>>::deserialize(deserializer)?.unwrap_or_default();
    Ok(entries
        .into_iter()
        .filter_map(|entry| {
            let id = entry.get("id")?.as_str().filter(|id| !id.is_empty())?;
            let name = entry.get("name").and_then(Value::as_str)
                .filter(|name| !name.is_empty()).unwrap_or(id);
            Some(RegistrationRoom { id: id.to_owned(), name: name.to_owned() })
        })
        .collect())
}
```

A room with no id cannot be stored or addressed, so it is dropped; a room with no name takes its id. Failing the whole building over one unusable room would deny a building for a room nobody asked about.

.box {maxTemperature is read but never sent} type:{note}
    telemetry seeds a building temperature bound from it. digital-twin does not populate it — it syncs thresholds over HTTP (`service/buildings.rs::clone_thresholds`) instead. Typing the payload is what made that visible; the field stays `Option` rather than being deleted, because the consumer's behaviour is unchanged and a replayed older message may still carry it.

`name` is `#[serde(default)]` rather than required, so an empty one reaches telemetry's own check and produces its exact message — `name: must be a non-empty string.` That message is not decoration: it travels back on the completion event and becomes the failure reason on the user's upload.

---

## 2. `RegistrationCompleted` — an unknown status is a failure

```rust
pub struct RegistrationCompleted {
    pub building_id: String,
    pub status: String,
    pub error: Option<String>,
}

impl RegistrationCompleted {
    pub fn ready(building_id: &str) -> Self { /* status: "ready" */ }
    pub fn failed(building_id: &str, error: &str) -> Self { /* status: "failed" */ }

    pub fn failure(&self) -> Option<String> {
        (!self.is_ready())
            .then(|| self.error.clone().unwrap_or_else(|| self.status.clone()))
    }
}
```

`status` stays a `String` rather than becoming an enum on purpose. An enum would make an unrecognised status a *parse* failure, and a parse failure on this topic means the consumer logs and skips — leaving the user's upload stuck at "pending" forever. `failure()` instead treats anything that is not `ready` as failed, naming the unrecognised status as the reason.

---

## 3. `WifiObservations` — one building tick of Wi-Fi sightings

ap-collector builds it in Python, telemetry checks it, counts phones per router room and republishes the received bytes, digital-twin places the dots.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant C as ap-collector
        participant TS as telemetry
        participant K as Kafka
        participant T as digital-twin

        C->>TS: POST signed tick
        TS->>K: wifi-observations, keyed by buildingId, bytes as received
        K->>T: consume live, recheck, keep the newest per building

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `buildingId` | signing building |
| `tick` | snapshot time, Unix ms |
| `answered` | router sensorIds that replied, each once; a missing router failed |
| `connected` | `{token, router, signal}` per router listing a phone; one token on two rows is one phone |
| `bursts` | `{token, started, heard: {router: dBm}}`; always present, empty when probes are off |
| `devicesPerPerson` | optional site factor, above 0; telemetry derives `ratioDeviceCount` from it, absent means no estimate |

- **No MAC, no coordinates.** Tokens are 16 hex characters minted at random on site.
- **Bound by**: `schemas/fixtures/wifi-observations.json` (cases + `rejected`), `schemas/json/wifi-observations.schema.json`, `twin-schema/tests/wifi_observations_{schema,conformance}.rs`, ap-collector `tests/test_observations_contract.py`.
- **Limits**: at most 2000 `connected` rows (`MAX_CONNECTED`), 2000 `bursts` (`MAX_BURSTS`), 1 to 6 routers per burst (`MAX_HEARD`), so a tick of UUID router ids stays under telemetry's 1 MiB body limit.
- **Refused by the type**: unknown fields (`deny_unknown_fields`), since telemetry republishes the bytes and an unchecked field would travel with them.
- **Refused by `problems()`**: what serde cannot check: token pattern, signal range, a router answering twice, `devicesPerPerson` not above 0, the limits. Telemetry calls it before publishing; digital-twin may trust what it consumes.

---

## 4. `Devices` — one building's estimated devices, for the browser

digital-twin places the devices from each fresh tick and publishes them; socket routes them to the building's room.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant T as digital-twin
        participant R as Redis
        participant S as socket
        participant B as browser

        T->>R: PUBLISH twin:devices:{buildingId}
        R->>S: pattern twin:devices:*
        S->>B: devices event, room building:{buildingId}, bytes as received

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `buildingId` | the tick's building; must equal the channel's |
| `tick` | the collector snapshot placed, Unix ms; the browser drops devices older than 30 s |
| `devices` | every device placed this tick; always present, empty clears the building |
| `floor`, `room` | floor index (lowest first) and room id |
| `x`, `y`, `z` | position in twin metres; `y` is the floor's level |
| `radius` | one spread of the position, metres |
| `connected` | false for someone passing who never joined the Wi-Fi |

- **Channel names are functions**: `DEVICES_CHANNEL_PREFIX`, `DEVICES_CHANNEL_PATTERN`, `devices_channel(building)`, `building_of_devices_channel(channel)` (`None` for any other channel).
- **No token, no MAC, no id.** `deny_unknown_fields` on `Device`, so socket skips a device carrying one.
- **Nothing is stored**: Redis pub/sub only; no Timescale, Mongo or Kafka copy.
- **Bound by**: `schemas/fixtures/devices.json` (cases + `rejected`), `schemas/json/devices.schema.json`, `twin-schema/tests/devices_{schema,conformance}.rs`, twin's `redis_devices.rs`, socket's `relay.rs`.

---

## 5. `EstimatedOccupancy` — digital-twin's room counts, for telemetry to store

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `buildingId` | the tick's building |
| `tick` | the collector snapshot, Unix ms; each reading's timestamp |
| `rooms` | room id → connected devices placed in it |

- **Topic `ESTIMATED_OCCUPANCY_TOPIC`** (`estimated-occupancy`), keyed by `buildingId`, 1 h retention, created by digital-twin.
- **Sent per accepted tick with at least one router answering**; nothing when none did, since zeros would record an empty building.
- **Every room on a floor with a placed router**, zeros included. A floor with none is left out: twin can place no one there.
- **Connected devices only.** A passing device is placed too rarely to count.
- **Counts only**: `deny_unknown_fields`, so no position can ride along.
- **Never `totalDeviceCount`**: telemetry stores it as its own metric, `estimatedOccupancy`.
- **Bound by**: `schemas/fixtures/estimated-occupancy.json`, `schemas/json/estimated-occupancy.schema.json`, `twin-schema/tests/estimated_occupancy_{schema,conformance}.rs`, telemetry's `occupancy_consumer.rs` and `kernel/occupancy.rs`.

---

## 6. What is deliberately not in this crate

`Building`, `Room`, `Coordinates` and `Dimensions` stay in `digital-twin/src/domain/`. The issue that prompted this crate assumed telemetry parsed twin's building payload over HTTP; it does not — its only call, `GET /domain/{id}`, returns a bare `Vec<String>` of domain names. Moving twin's own domain types into a shared crate would have produced a dependency with exactly one consumer.

The services that *do* read the full building are `agent` (Python) and the frontend (TypeScript), and no Rust crate can help either. What holds those three in line is a fixture:

| Language | Asserts |
|---|---|
| Rust | `digital-twin/tests/building_conformance.rs` — parses `schemas/fixtures/building.json` into the real `Building`, then round-trips it back to JSON and compares |
| Python | `agent/tests/unit/test_building_conformance.py` — runs the same file through `_room_payload`/`_building_payload`, the projections the twin tools hand to the model |

The round-trip half is the one that matters: it fails when twin's type stops carrying a field the fixture has, which is precisely the drift a Python consumer would otherwise discover as a `None` inside a tool result.
